// @test: typecheck-fail
// @level: L0
// @tier: 0
// @tags: modules, name-resolution, module-path, arity
// @expected-error: E102
//
// Pin (T0806, second half) — a call through a module path must satisfy the
// callee's arity.
//
// This is a SEPARATE defect from the missing-name half, and it fails at a
// length where the name half already works:
//
//     fn f(a: Int); f()       local call, too few    E102
//     fn f(a: Int); f(1,2,3)  local call, too many   E102
//     m.absent()              module path, no such name, 2 segments   E100
//
//     m.f()                   module path, too few, 2 segments   silent -> nil
//     a.b.f()                 module path, too few, 3 segments   silent -> nil
//
// So at two segments the name is resolved and the arity of the very same
// call is not. Fixing the length threshold alone leaves this in place.
//
// It has a practical consequence for anyone probing the other half: an
// oracle built as "assign the result to a deliberately wrong type" cannot
// tell a live path from a dead one when the arity is wrong, because both
// sides are silent. A batch of 22 such probes in one file reported exactly
// one error — from the single control whose zero arity happened to match.
// That reads like stop-after-first-error, and is not: three errors in one
// file are all three reported.
//
// `outer.inner.takes_one(1)` is called first and must keep working; the
// companion control `module_path_call_control.vr` pins the good path on its
// own so a fix cannot pass by rejecting module-path calls wholesale.

module outer {
    public module inner {
        public fn takes_one(a: Int) -> Int { a }
    }
}

fn main() {
    let good: Int = outer.inner.takes_one(1);
    let bad: Int = outer.inner.takes_one();
    print(f"{good} {bad}");
}
